"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][38]={"university_physics_en:M38-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M38-L1 – Seismic Refraction and Two-Layer Traveltimes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: wave mechanics; M31: inverse\nproblems; M37: astrophysics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare direct and critically\nrefracted waves in a horizontal two-layer model, distinguishing the\nmeaning of an intercept and the minimum offset.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Seismic traveltimes constrain velocities and interfaces in the\nsubsurface. Our toy model assumes two homogeneous isotropic layers with\na horizontal boundary, with the lower layer faster than the upper one. A\ncritically refracted head wave can form beyond a minimum source–receiver\noffset. The result is an inference from travel times rather than a\ndirect photograph of subsurface geometry.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2&gt;v_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninterface depth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand incidence angle from the interface normal,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin i_c=v_1/v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe direct arrival obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_d=x/v_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRay theory gives the head-wave arrival\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_h(x)=2H\\cos i_c/v_1+x/v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut only for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge 2H\\tan i_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe line extrapolates to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_i=2H\\cos i_c/v_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere that head-wave path does not exist. The critical offset and the\nfirst-arrival crossover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\times}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_d=t_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nare different.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_h(x)=\\frac{2H\\cos i_c}{v_1}+\\frac{x}{v_2},\\qquad \\sin i_c=\\frac{v_1}{v_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For the constructed model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=2\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=4\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=1\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_c=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.866<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_i\\approx0.866\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.155<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\min}\\approx1.155\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.464<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\times}\\approx3.464\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_d=2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.866<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_h\\approx1.866\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe head wave arrives first. The plot separates the extrapolated line\nfrom its physical domain.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_261_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Traveltime versus offset with direct and critically refracted arrivals, distinguishing physical onset from first-arrival crossover.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Traveltime versus offset with direct and\ncritically refracted arrivals, distinguishing physical onset from\nfirst-arrival crossover.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic traveltime calculation for a two-layer geophysical\nmodel.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>These are not measurements. The dashed straight line is a nonphysical\nextrapolation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=2\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same velocities.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which arrival is earlier at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_h(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot a physical head-wave travel time?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.732<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_i\\approx1.732\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nproportional to layer depth.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_d=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.366<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_h\\approx1.366\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndirect arrival.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The critical-refraction path along the lower interface requires a\nfinite minimum offset.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The two-layer ray model ignores interface dip, lateral variation,\nanisotropy, attenuation and finite wavelength. Traveltime inversion is\nnonunique; hidden layers can evade simple refraction surveys.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Large-scale geophysics also studies rotating fluids, motivating\ngeostrophic balance. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://uw-geophysics-edu.github.io/ess314/lectures/06_seismic_refraction_I.html\">University of Washington – Seismic Refraction I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Two-layer geometry, critical angle, minimum offset and traveltime curves; original worked example; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L1 – Seismic Refraction and Two-Layer Traveltimes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.eoas.ubc.ca/courses/eosc350/content/methods/meth_6/horizontal.html\">University of British Columbia – Seismic Refraction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Traveltime interpretation and assumptions of horizontal interfaces; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L1 – Seismic Refraction and Two-Layer Traveltimes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M38-L2 – Geostrophic Wind and Rotating Flows<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: fluid mechanics; M19: rotating\nframes; M38-L1 · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Balance horizontal pressure\ngradients against the Coriolis term, and identify limits near the\nequator and in frictional layers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Earth rotation deflects large-scale atmospheric and oceanic flow. In\neast–north–up coordinates, ideal geostrophic horizontal velocity follows\nisobars. This is not an extra physical force or a model of every gust;\nit is a steady momentum balance when friction and local acceleration are\nsmall.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v_g=(u_g,v_g,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2\\Omega\\sin\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the horizontal pressure gradient, geostrophic balance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\,\\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf v_g=-\\nabla_h p/\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g=-\\partial_y p/(\\rho f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_g=\\partial_x p/(\\rho f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA northward pressure decrease yields an eastward geostrophic wind in the\nNorthern Hemisphere, where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVertical hydrostatic balance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_z p=-\\rho g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a distinct approximation, not another form of the horizontal\nrelation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\,\\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf v_g=-\\frac{\\nabla_h p}{\\rho},\\qquad u_g=-\\frac{\\partial_y p}{\\rho f}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1.2\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_y p=-1.2\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g=+10\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe positive sign means east. The Coriolis acceleration points south and\nbalances the northward pressure-gradient acceleration. The diagram shows\ndirections, not operational weather data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_115_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Horizontal isobars and direction arrows for pressure-gradient force, Coriolis term and eastward geostrophic wind.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Horizontal isobars and direction arrows\nfor pressure-gradient force, Coriolis term and eastward geostrophic\nwind.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Vector balance in Northern Hemisphere east–north coordinates; not\nto scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed pressure gradient?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where does the pressure-gradient force point when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases northward?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can the geostrophic formula not be evaluated by dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the equator?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its magnitude halves, with sign determined by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Northward, opposite to the pressure gradient.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The dominant balance changes; both the approximation and division by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfail.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This balance is most appropriate for large-scale, rotation-dominated,\nnearly frictionless flow away from the equator. Boundary layers, fronts\nand strong curvature require ageostrophic terms. Signs assume the stated\ncoordinate convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Rotation and conducting fluid flow also matter for the geomagnetic\nfield. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=Geostrophic+Wind\">NOAA National Weather Service – Geostrophic Wind<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Definition of geostrophic wind and flow parallel to isobars; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L2 – Geostrophic Wind and Rotating Flows · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.weather.gov/source/zhu/ZHU_Training_Page/clouds/planetary_boundary_layer/PBL.html\">NOAA National Weather Service – Planetary Boundary Layer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Boundary-layer friction and limitations of geostrophic balance; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L2 – Geostrophic Wind and Rotating Flows · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M38-L3 – Geodynamo, Magnetic Induction and Diffusion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: magnetic fields; M31: partial\ndifferential equations; M38-L2 · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish advection, field\nstretching and magnetic diffusion, and compute a magnetic Reynolds\nnumber for an illustrative model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Much of Earth’s main magnetic field is produced by electric currents\nin its conducting liquid outer core. Thermal and chemical buoyancy drive\nflow, while rotation helps organize it. Dynamo theory asks under what\nconditions flow can sustain magnetic fields. One measured field map\nalone does not uniquely recover core flow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an incompressible conducting fluid with constant magnetic\ndiffusivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=1/(\\mu_0\\sigma)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe induction equation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t\\mathbf B=\\nabla\\times(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf B)+\\eta\\nabla^2\\mathbf B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsubject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first right-hand term describes transport and stretching; the second\nrepresents resistive diffusion. The dimensionless ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m=UL/\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompares them at typical scales\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U,L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA large value alone does not prove self-sustained dynamo action.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>η<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf B}{\\partial t}=\\nabla\\times(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf B)+\\eta\\nabla^2\\mathbf B,\\qquad R_m=\\frac{UL}{\\eta}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Using deliberately constructed scales\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=10^6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=2\\,\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe advective timescale is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L/U=10^{10}\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile diffusion takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^2/\\eta=5\\times10^{11}\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not predict the real terrestrial field strength.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_113_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic core with conducting circulation, magnetic field lines and a diffusion symbol; not a measured map.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic core with conducting\ncirculation, magnetic field lines and a diffusion symbol; not a measured\nmap.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Induction versus diffusion in an idealized dynamo.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not a reconstruction of actual core flow or field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the SI units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot automatically imply a dynamo instability?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to 25.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a scaling ratio; geometry, boundaries, feedback and flow\nstructure also matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The toy model assumes constant conductivity and scalar diffusivity.\nReal core flow is turbulent, multiscale and magnetohydrodynamically\ncoupled. Reversals cannot be predicted from this single equation without\nflow and thermodynamic dynamics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>From the deep Earth we move to energy exchange at the top of the\natmosphere. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/geomagnetism/introduction-geomagnetism\">U.S. Geological Survey – Introduction to Geomagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Convective motion in the conducting outer core and magnetic field generation; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L3 – Geodynamo, Magnetic Induction and Diffusion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M38-L4 – Global Radiation Budget and Climate Forcing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M22: thermodynamics; M32: measurement\nmethods; M38-L2: atmospheric flow · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Balance global top-of-atmosphere\nradiative fluxes, distinguishing positive net downward energy uptake\nfrom radiative equilibrium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A climate system receives shortwave solar radiation, reflects some of\nit, and emits longwave thermal radiation. At the top of the atmosphere\n(TOA), net flux diagnoses the energy budget but is not itself the\nnear-surface temperature change. The atmosphere, surface and ocean can\nstore and redistribute energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npositive downward:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=(1-A)S_0/4-\\mathrm{OLR}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis planetary albedo,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe incoming solar irradiance at the assumed orbit, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{OLR}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe global mean outgoing longwave flux. The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narises from the ratio of a sphere’s projected area to its surface area.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe mean TOA radiation budget is balanced;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates net energy gain but not an instantaneous specified\nwarming.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(positive downward)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=\\frac{(1-A)S_0}{4}-\\mathrm{OLR}\\qquad\\text{(positive downward)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A constructed budget with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0=1360\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>237<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{OLR}=237\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives absorbed shortwave radiation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">238\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=+1\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are educational inputs, not current satellite observations. The\ngraphic indicates the flux directions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_297_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic TOA budget showing absorbed solar shortwave energy and outgoing longwave radiation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic TOA budget showing absorbed\nsolar shortwave energy and outgoing longwave radiation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic radiation budget at the top of the atmosphere.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined positive downward; all numerical values are constructed\nteaching inputs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{OLR}=238\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith all other inputs fixed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does increasing albedo to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.31<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.31<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nholding other inputs fixed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is global mean solar irradiance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absorption falls by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.4\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=-2.4\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Intercepted power scales with cross-sectional area, averaged over\nthe full spherical surface.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>A global TOA budget suppresses spectral absorption, spatial/seasonal\nheterogeneity and cloud dynamics. Geostrophy models horizontal flow, not\nthe global energy-budget term. Radiation transfer is addressed\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>How energy flux relates to measured radiance is a radiation-transfer\nquestion. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/chapter-7/\">IPCC AR6 WG I – Chapter 7, Earth Energy Budget and Feedbacks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Radiative forcing, energy balance and feedback; course sign convention defined explicitly; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L4 – Global Radiation Budget and Climate Forcing · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M38-L5 – Radiative Transfer, Optical Depth and Emission<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M20: optics; M22: thermal radiation;\nM38-L4 · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Integrate a scalar directional\nradiative-transfer equation and separate assumptions about absorption,\nlocal thermal emission and absence of scattering.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Spectral radiance changes as light passes through matter because of\nabsorption, emission and possibly scattering. Our restricted toy case\nuses an isothermal, non-scattering slab with a constant source function.\nOptical depth is then a dimensionless quantity measured along the chosen\npropagation path, not a geographic height or universal cloud\nproperty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreasing along the radiation path:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dI_\\nu/d\\tau=-I_\\nu+S_\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor local thermodynamic equilibrium (LTE), thermal emission and no\nscattering, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_\\nu=B_\\nu(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt constant temperature across optical thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{out}}=I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{in}}e^{-\\tau_*}+B_\\nu(T)(1-e^{-\\tau_*})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDirectional path length must be incorporated in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith scattering or non-LTE,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerally differs from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{out}}=I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{in}}e^{-\\tau_*}+B_\\nu(T)(1-e^{-\\tau_*}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a constructed monochromatic case with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{in}}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_\\nu=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narbitrary radiance units and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.693<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_*=\\ln 2\\approx0.693<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits. As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_*\\rightarrow0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe slab adds negligible emission; at large thickness the emergent\nradiance approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe drawing separates contributions at one frequency.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_236_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Radiation passing through an isothermal slab: attenuated incident radiance plus a thermal source contribution.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Radiation passing through an isothermal\nslab: attenuated incident radiance plus a thermal source\ncontribution.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Single-frequency LTE model without scattering, not a measured\natmospheric spectrum.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{out}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_*=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor arbitrary incident radiance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the limit at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_*\\rightarrow\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the isothermal slab?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must strong scattering not be handled by this unchanged\nrelation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{out}}=I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{in}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\nu,\\mathrm{out}}\\rightarrow B_\\nu(T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Scattering couples directions and changes the source function.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The expression is spectral, homogeneous, isothermal, locally thermal\nand non-scattering. A grey model averages over spectral features; it is\nnot an exact description of greenhouse-gas line absorption. Other texts\nmay use the opposite path/optical-depth convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Observations of radiative fields help constrain model states; data\nassimilation formalizes this. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-815-atmospheric-radiation-fall-2008/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atmospheric Radiation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Teaching context for atmospheric radiative transfer; scalar equation independently modelled; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L5 – Radiative Transfer, Optical Depth and Emission · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M38-L6 – Four-Dimensional Variational Assimilation and Covariances<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31: inverse problems and numerics;\nM32: measurement errors; M38-L5 · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret a quadratic 4D-Var\nobjective under linearization and compute a scalar state correction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Numerical weather prediction requires a coherent initial model state.\nObservations are incomplete and noisy; a background state from a prior\nforecast is also uncertain. Variational assimilation combines them under\nassumed error statistics. In 4D-Var observations at several times\nconstrain a model trajectory within a finite window.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an increment\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbackground-error covariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand observation-error covariances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet the innovation be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_i=y_i-H_iM_i(x_b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith tangent-linear mapping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi>M<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i=H_i\\,M_i^\\prime<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nminimize\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\delta x)=\\tfrac12\\delta x^TB^{-1}\\delta x+\\tfrac12\\sum_i(d_i-U_i\\delta x)^TR_i^{-1}(d_i-U_i\\delta x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf covariances are positive definite, the Hessian of this quadratic\napproximation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msubsup><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2J=B^{-1}+\\sum_iU_i^TR_i^{-1}U_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is an incremental mathematical framework, not a claim about a\nspecific current ECMWF implementation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\delta x)=\\frac12\\delta x^TB^{-1}\\delta x+\\frac12\\sum_i(d_i-U_i\\delta x)^TR_i^{-1}(d_i-U_i\\delta x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In an original one-dimensional one-time example set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(x)=x^2/8+(2-x)^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nminimized at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_a=1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith Hessian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\prime\\prime=1/4+1=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe analysis variance implied by this quadratic model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/J\\prime\\prime=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe chart shows two weighted terms and their sum.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_103_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Quadratic objective with background and observation terms and minimum at x equal to 1.6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Quadratic objective with background and\nobservation terms and minimum at x equal to 1.6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Constructed scalar example.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Neither weather data nor an operational 4D-Var product.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the minimum for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=R=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naffect observation weight?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nneed positive definiteness for the displayed inverses?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe weighted mean.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The observation receives less weight.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">To guarantee inverses and positive quadratic curvature in this\nsimple model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Operational assimilation includes nonlinear models, error\ncorrelations, observation QC and approximate covariances. The given\nHessian belongs only to the linearized quadratic problem. No current\noperational assimilation window or ensemble size is assumed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>With an estimated initial state we can model the response to\nradiative forcing. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ecmwf.int/sites/default/files/elibrary/2023/81368-ifs-documentation-cy48r1-part-ii-data-assimilation.pdf\">ECMWF – 4D Variational Assimilation, IFS Documentation CY48R1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · General 4D-Var cost-function structure; not a claim about the current operating configuration; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L6 – Four-Dimensional Variational Assimilation and Covariances · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ecmwf.int/en/newsletter/175/earth-system-science/linearised-physics-heart-ecmwfs-4d-var\">ECMWF – Linearised Physics: the Heart of ECMWF’s 4D-Var<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Covariances, tangent-linear/adjoint models and incremental approach; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L6 – Four-Dimensional Variational Assimilation and Covariances · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M38-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M38-L7 – Climate Feedback and Equilibrium Climate Sensitivity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M38 – Master: Geophysics, Atmospheric Physics and Climate Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M22: thermal balance; M38-L4: TOA\nenergy budget; M38-L6 · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate radiative forcing to a\npositive restoring-feedback convention and compute an idealized\nequilibrium warming.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Additional radiative forcing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nperturbs the global TOA budget. Temperature, water vapour, clouds and\nalbedo then alter the net energy flux. Equilibrium climate sensitivity\ndescribes long-term global-mean surface warming from CO₂ doubling under\na stated model/assessment definition. It is neither the instantaneous\nenergy imbalance nor a single directly measured parameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We define net flux\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npositive downward, as in M38-L4, and choose a <strong>positive restoring\ncoefficient<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=F-\\lambda\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mathematical equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T_{\\mathrm{eq}}=F/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIPCC literature also uses a feedback parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the opposite sign:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=-\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same local linear balance. Both conventions agree if signs and\nunits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}\\,K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremain explicit.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>F<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=F-\\lambda\\Delta T,\\qquad \\Delta T_{\\mathrm{eq}}=\\frac{F}{\\lambda}\\quad(\\lambda&gt;0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For illustrative forcing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=3.6\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1.2\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}\\,K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nequilibrium warming would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T_{\\mathrm{eq}}=3.0\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a transient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=1\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe remaining imbalance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=2.4\\,\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese parameters are deliberately chosen and are not new IPCC estimates.\nThe plot shows the linear forcing–response relation and its equilibrium\npoint.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_040_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear relation between downward-positive TOA net flux and temperature response, crossing zero at 3 K.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear relation between downward-positive\nTOA net flux and temperature response, crossing zero at 3\nK.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustrative values and constant positive restoring\ncoefficient.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No fit to climate observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What equilibrium warming results if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What sign has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the indicated IPCC convention?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot describe the instant when forcing first begins?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=-\\lambda&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a stabilizing response.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ocean and other reservoirs delay adjustment; immediately after\nperturbation the flux imbalance is generally nonzero.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This one-parameter linear model cannot replace coupled climate\nmodels. Feedback may depend on climate state, timescale, warming pattern\nand forcing type. The constructed 3 K must not be presented as a\nstandalone IPCC range or an inference about contemporary observed\nwarming.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M38 connects seismic and magnetic models to rotating fluids,\nradiation, assimilation and climate feedbacks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/chapter-7/\">IPCC AR6 WG I – Chapter 7, Earth Energy Budget and Feedbacks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Radiative forcing, energy balance and feedback; course sign convention defined explicitly; Subject relevance to lesson: M38-L7 – Climate Feedback and Equilibrium Climate Sensitivity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
